# _headers — Cloudflare Pages response headers. SPEC §17 A4.
#
# The project had no _headers file at all until 2026-07-25. This is a static
# site, so the exposure was modest — but a CSP is a handful of lines and it is
# the difference between an injection being a catastrophe and a non-event.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT COMPILED OR VALIDATED BY ANYTHING. A typo does not fail the
# build; it fails silently and the header is simply absent.
#
# ===> THE CSP IS ENFORCED. IT SHIPPED REPORT-ONLY FIRST, ON PURPOSE. <===
# A wrong CSP does not degrade the app, it BREAKS it — for everyone, at once,
# on the one deploy nobody is watching. Report-Only logs violations and blocks
# nothing, so the first deploy cost a console read instead of an outage. It has
# now been flipped to `Content-Security-Policy` and blocks for real.
#
# IF THIS EVER NEEDS CHANGING AGAIN, GO BACK TO REPORT-ONLY FIRST. The ladder
# is: add the entry Report-Only, watch one clean session at both widths with a
# storm selected and imagery on, then flip. Editing an ENFORCED policy directly
# skips the rung that exists to catch the mistake.
#
# `node tools/csp-check.mjs` boots the app locally under this exact policy and
# fails on any violation. It cannot cover what needs live data — a selected
# storm's imagery — so it is a floor, not a proof.
#
# The connect-src list was built by reading the actual fetch call sites and
# ENDPOINT constants, not from memory.
#
# ===> IT SHRANK BY FOUR HOSTS IN §17 PASS B, AND THE SHRINK IS THE POINT. <===
# mapservices.weather.noaa.gov and www.gdacs.org used to be fetched DIRECTLY by
# the client; Pass B put both behind the /api/ relay for load reasons, so the
# browser no longer talks to either. www.nhc.noaa.gov and ftp.nhc.noaa.gov were
# never client-fetched at all — they were relayed from the beginning and were
# listed here defensively. pub-...r2.dev is read by functions/tiles/[[path]].js
# and reaches the browser as same-origin /tiles/, so it was never needed either.
#
# THIS IS THE PAYOFF OF THE ORIGIN COLLAPSE THAT IS EASY TO MISS: a policy that
# only permits what is actually used is the difference between a CSP that
# documents the app and a CSP that would still wave through a compromised
# dependency phoning a federal endpoint. Every upstream host removed here is
# one fewer place an injection can reach. If a fetch to one of these ever
# returns, it is a bug in the relay, not a missing CSP entry — fix the fetch.
#
# ===> AND IT SHRANK BY TWO MORE ON 2026-07-26. <===
# gibs.earthdata.nasa.gov and view.eumetsat.int are gone for the same reason the
# four before them went: satellite imagery now goes through
# /api/imagery/satellite, so the browser no longer talks to either vendor. That
# move was made for CACHING, not for CSP — GIBS forbids caching outright and
# answers between 0.8 and 30.7 seconds (measured) — but the policy shrinking is
# the same payoff, banked again. The satellite hosts are contacted by
# functions/api/imagery/satellite.js, which runs server-side where a page CSP
# does not apply.
#
# THE BROWSER NOW REACHES EXACTLY ONE THIRD-PARTY HOST. If a fetch to any
# satellite vendor ever reappears in a CSP report, it is a bug in the relay path
# — probably a fallback somebody added — not a missing entry here. Fix the fetch.
# (There is deliberately no client-side fallback to the vendors: see the note in
# functions/api/imagery/satellite.js.)
#
# What is LEFT is everything the browser genuinely still contacts:
#   tiles.openfreemap.org     basemap tiles + storm-name glyphs (§11)
#   'self' / blob: / data:    our own origin, MapLibre's workers, canvas reads

/*
  # --- Content Security Policy (REPORT-ONLY — see the note above) ----------
  # 'unsafe-inline' on style-src is REQUIRED and not laziness: MapLibre GL
  # writes inline styles on the canvas and its controls at runtime, and
  # index.html carries the first-paint token block (the CSS-cannot-import-JS
  # fallback documented in its <style>). Removing it breaks the map.
  #
  # script-src has NO 'unsafe-inline' and NO 'unsafe-eval'. Every script is a
  # file on our own origin, with ONE exception, and the exception is pinned by
  # hash rather than allowed by category:
  #
  #   sha256-GF0Gr6Uz2bjBfDxandl5dJHz1yt4q02oZTUpLffL0jw=
  #     The pre-paint theme resolver in index.html (light mode, 2026-07-26). It
  #     has to run BEFORE the first paint or a light-mode device flashes the
  #     dark globe on every cold load — which is a module import too late. A
  #     hash permits that exact script and nothing else, so this is strictly
  #     stronger than 'unsafe-inline' and no weaker than 'self' alone.
  #
  # THE HASH IS LOAD-BEARING AND FAILS CLOSED. Edit that script — even the
  # indentation — and it stops matching, the resolver stops running, and the
  # only symptom is a flash on load plus a CSP report nobody reads.
  # `node tools/csp-hash-check.mjs` recomputes it and is what stops that
  # shipping. Run it whenever index.html changes.
  #
  # Any FURTHER inline script should go in a module instead. One pinned
  # exception with a reason is a policy; three is 'unsafe-inline' spelled out
  # the long way.
  #
  # blob: on worker-src and connect-src is MapLibre's own plumbing — it builds
  # its tile workers from blobs. Not optional.
  # ===> THE TWO CLOUDFLARE RUM HOSTS ARE ALLOWED, NOT BLOCKED. <===
  # Cloudflare INJECTS its Web Analytics beacon into every response; it is not
  # in index.html and there is no dashboard switch to stop it (the Pages
  # project has no such row, and account-level Manage-site defers back to
  # Pages). So the choice was never "beacon or no beacon" — it was "beacon that
  # works, or beacon that fires a CSP violation on every single load".
  #
  #   static.cloudflareinsights.com   the injected beacon script  -> script-src
  #   cloudflareinsights.com          POST /cdn-cgi/rum           -> connect-src
  #
  # It also earns the two entries. Web Analytics' Debug View names the exact
  # ELEMENT behind a slow interaction — `canvas.maplibregl-canvas`,
  # `button.nudge-action` — which lib/perf.js cannot do at any price. Different
  # instrument, not a duplicate.
  #
  # ===> AND THE POLICY IS NOW ENFORCED. <===
  # It shipped Report-Only first so a wrong policy would cost a console read
  # instead of an outage, and it has now run clean. Report-Only that never gets
  # flipped is a comment, not a control — it blocks nothing, so every day it
  # stays that way is a day the app has a security header it does not have.
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com 'sha256-GF0Gr6Uz2bjBfDxandl5dJHz1yt4q02oZTUpLffL0jw='; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; font-src 'self' https://tiles.openfreemap.org; connect-src 'self' blob: data: https://tiles.openfreemap.org https://cloudflareinsights.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; child-src 'self' blob:; manifest-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests

  # --- The rest. These CANNOT break a static map app, so they enforce now. --
  # frame-ancestors above is the modern clickjacking control; X-Frame-Options
  # is the older header kept for browsers that predate it.
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  # Deny the capability APIs this app has no business touching. GEOLOCATION IS
  # DELIBERATELY ALLOWED (self) — it is how §8's home is set. Everything else
  # is off, so a compromised dependency cannot reach for a camera or a mic.
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(self), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=(), magnetometer=(), gyroscope=(), accelerometer=()

# Vendored libraries carry their version in the FILENAME (§17 A3), so the URL
# can never mean something new and the browser may keep it forever. Same
# reasoning the service worker uses to treat /vendor/ as cache-first.
/vendor/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# ===> THE SETTLED HISTORY. SAME REASONING AS /vendor/* ABOVE. <===
# `seasons/data/` holds NOAA's HURDAT2 files — 6.8 MB Atlantic, 3.9 MB Pacific
# — and their filenames carry BOTH the last season and NOAA's revision stamp:
# `hurdat2-atlantic-2025-02272026.txt`. So the URL can never mean something new
# and the browser may keep it forever. §57.35 FIX 11, SPEC-OPS.md §18.8.
#
# ===> THE REVISION IS IN THE NAME AND FIX 11 DID NOT ASK FOR IT. <===
# FIX 11 said the SEASON is the cache bust. That is right until NOAA revises a
# season it has already published, which it does — the real directory carries
# five revisions of the 2022 Atlantic file. Under season-only naming every one
# of them would write to this same immutable URL, and a browser that fetched
# the April revision would keep it forever and never see the May correction.
#
# LISTED AS `/seasons/data/*` RATHER THAN `/seasons/*` ON PURPOSE. The index
# next door must stay revalidating, and two rules whose paths overlap is a
# question about precedence that this file — which nothing validates — is the
# worst possible place to have. The two paths do not overlap.
/seasons/data/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The one MUTABLE file in the whole feature, and its whole job is to be mutable.
# It names which HURDAT2 file to fetch, so next February's swap is a data commit
# rather than a code change and a deploy. A stale copy here points the app at a
# file that has just been deleted, which is a 404 on the entire history — so it
# revalidates with everything else.
/seasons/index.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# The storm-to-report lookup (§57.22a). It sits with the DATA in spirit and
# with the INDEX in caching: it is regenerated by a monthly job whenever NOAA
# publishes a report, so a copy held for a year would leave a reader with no
# link on a storm that has one — the "no report was written for this storm"
# sentence, said about a report that exists. Revalidates, like index.json and
# for the same reason.
/seasons/reports.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# The Wall of Years (§57.36) — every season reduced to four numbers a storm,
# 46 KB, rewritten by the same monthly job that refreshes HURDAT2. Its name
# carries no revision stamp, unlike everything under `seasons/data/`, so it is
# the one file in the feature that can change meaning without changing name.
# Held immutable it would show last February's history for a year.
/seasons/wall.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# ===> THE ARCHIVE'S CODE, LISTED FILE BY FILE, AND THE UGLINESS IS THE POINT.
# `seasons/` is the one directory in this repo that holds BOTH kinds of thing:
# `seasons/data/*` is NOAA's history, immutable forever (above), and these four
# are ordinary application code that must revalidate like every other module.
#
# SO IT CANNOT BE `/seasons/*`. That would overlap the immutable rule above,
# and this file — which nothing validates and which fails silently — is the
# worst possible place to be relying on a precedence rule between two matching
# patterns. `/seasons/*.js` would avoid the overlap, but Cloudflare's own
# _headers documentation does not state that a wildcard may carry a suffix, and
# a rule that silently matches nothing is exactly the "no header at all" case
# the block above was written to fix.
#
# ==> A NEW FILE UNDER `seasons/` NEEDS A LINE HERE. Nothing catches the
# omission, and the symptom is a module that silently goes stale. <==
/seasons/index.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/seasons/bar.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/seasons/deep-link.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/seasons/seasons.css
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# The service worker must never be served stale — a cached sw.js is how a bad
# worker outlives the fix for it.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# The shell is network-first in the worker; keep the HTTP layer honest too, so
# a deploy reaches a browser that has no worker yet.
/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# ===> OUR OWN MODULES. THE APP CAN RUN A MIXED VERSION WITHOUT THIS. <=========
# This file used to name exactly three things — index.html, sw.js and /vendor/*
# — and say NOTHING about the ~120 modules the app is actually made of.
# Measured on the live site: those modules come back with NO Cache-Control
# header at all. Not a wrong rule; no rule. A browser with no instruction is
# free to invent a lifetime and answer from disk without asking.
#
# THE PART THAT MAKES IT DANGEROUS RATHER THAN MERELY STALE: sw.js's
# networkFirst() calls plain fetch(), and fetch() consults the browser's HTTP
# cache BEFORE the network. So the worker believes it went to the network, the
# browser quietly answers from disk, and the guarantee the worker documents
# about itself is not true. The shell is pinned fresh above and is then free to
# import stale modules underneath it.
#
# Seen live: a storm rendered grey and correctly ended in the list while the
# globe drew her pink at full Cat-4 height. Two files, two versions, one
# screen — SPEC §9's two channels disagreeing about a hurricane, silently.
#
# `no-cache` does NOT mean "do not store". It means "store it, but revalidate
# before every use" — with an ETag that is a 304 and almost no bytes, which is
# why this is the same directive index.html and sw.js already carry rather than
# something weaker. Correctness first; the worker's own cache is what makes the
# app fast offline, and it is untouched by this.
#
# LISTED BY DIRECTORY, NOT AS A `/*.js` GLOB, so this can never reach into
# /vendor/* and undo the immutable rule above. A NEW TOP-LEVEL SOURCE DIRECTORY
# NEEDS A LINE HERE — nothing validates that, and the symptom of forgetting is
# a module that silently goes stale.
/main.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/pwa.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# ===> THE DIRECTORY THAT PROVED THE WARNING ABOVE IS REAL. <===
# `app/` was created after this block was written and nobody added a line for
# it, so its five modules — including app/views.js, which owns every drawer view
# — were shipping with NO cache instruction at all. Exactly the "mixed version"
# shape described above, in the composition layer, where a stale copy would
# disagree with the fresh data underneath it.
#
# Nothing catches this: `tools/load-probe.mjs` serves the real `_headers` and
# will show a module answering with no Cache-Control, but only if somebody
# looks. A NEW TOP-LEVEL SOURCE DIRECTORY STILL NEEDS A LINE HERE BY HAND.
/app/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/config/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/data/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/lib/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/map/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# ===> AND `proto/` PROVED IT A SECOND TIME. <===
# Same omission as `app/` above: the directory was created and no line was added
# here, so its five modules shipped with no cache instruction at all. It matters
# more now than it did — proto/shell.js imports config/, lib/ and map/, so a
# stale prototype can pair a fresh camera with an old shell and mislead a glass
# read about the very thing it exists to test.
/proto/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# ===> AND `tools/` IS THE THIRD TIME, CAUGHT BEFORE IT SHIPPED RATHER THAN
# AFTER. <=== It used to hold only Node scripts and one probe page, neither of
# which the browser fetched as a module. Browser-loaded probe modules changed
# that; `tools/imagery-probe.js` is the live one.
#
# A stale diagnostic is worse than a stale feature: a probe exists to be
# believed, and one answering from a browser's disk cache would report the
# previous run's world while looking exactly as authoritative.
/tools/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/ui/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# The manifest decides what an installed icon and name are. A stale one is not
# dangerous, but it is confusing in exactly the way a reinstall is supposed to
# cure, so it revalidates with everything else.
/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: no-cache
